Here's an example of an HTML page that compares two images using a slider:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Image Comparison Slider</title>
	<style>
		.container {
			position: relative;
			width: 100%;
			height: 500px;
			overflow: hidden;
		}

		.image {
			position: absolute;
			top: 0;
			bottom: 0;
			left: 0;
			right: 0;
			transition: all 0.5s;
		}

		.slider {
			position: absolute;
			top: 50%;
			left: 50%;
			transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
			z-index: 1;
			cursor: ew-resize;
		}

		.slider:before {
			content: '';
			position: absolute;
			top: 0;
			bottom: 0;
			left: 50%;
			width: 2px;
			background-color: #fff;
			z-index: 2;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<div class="container">
		<img class="image" src="image1.jpg">
		<img class="image" src="image2.jpg">
		<div class="slider"></div>
	</div>

	<script>
		const slider = document.querySelector('.slider');
		const images = document.querySelectorAll('.image');
		const container = document.querySelector('.container');
		const width = container.offsetWidth;

		slider.addEventListener('mousedown', function(e) {
			container.classList.add('active');
			slider.style.left = e.pageX - container.offsetLeft + 'px';

			const moveSlider = function(e) {
				slider.style.left = e.pageX - container.offsetLeft + 'px';

				let x = slider.offsetLeft;
				let ratio = x / width;

				images[0].style.width = ratio * 100 + '%';
				images[1].style.width = (1 - ratio) * 100 + '%';
			};

			document.addEventListener('mousemove', moveSlider);

			document.addEventListener('mouseup', function() {
				container.classList.remove('active');
				document.removeEventListener('mousemove', moveSlider);
			});
		});
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a container element with two image elements inside it and a slider element that acts as a handle. The images are positioned absolutely on top of each other, and the container has `overflow: hidden` so that only one of the images is visible at a time. The slider element is positioned in the middle of the container and has a background color that makes it visible.

When the user clicks and drags the slider element, the script calculates the position of the slider relative to the container and uses that to set the width of the two image elements. The left image's width is set to the ratio of the slider's position to the container's width, and the right image's width is set to the remaining width. The result is that the user can slide the handle to reveal more or less of each image.
